1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
It was a decade when technology rocked.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Creating a phone you didn't dial,

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
a watch that never ticked,

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
and a camera that captured a giant leap for mankind,

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
using the same power it takes to light a Christmas bulb.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Discover the secret under the hood of this Mustang rental

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
and find out what's inside the toys that etched

8
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
an enduring and super legacy.

9
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:33,000
Now, 60s Tech on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
In the words of the generation that defined it, the 1960s were groovy.

11
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:59,720
And if one 60s gadget traps the spirit of the time in a bottle,

12
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:03,020
it's this psychedelic icon, the lava lamp.

13
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:06,420
Part of its intrigue then and now

14
00:01:06,421 --> 00:01:12,280
is that the company that makes it won't divulge precisely what's inside that makes it work.

15
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:18,240
Employees at the lava lamp factory take an oath of secrecy when they're hired

16
00:01:18,241 --> 00:01:21,940
to ensure that the mysterious ingredients stay a mystery.

17
00:01:21,941 --> 00:01:23,940
We don't really know what's inside here,

18
00:01:23,941 --> 00:01:26,440
because it's a closely guarded trade secret.

19
00:01:26,441 --> 00:01:29,440
But what we do know is that the two substances

20
00:01:29,441 --> 00:01:34,440
are very close in density, and one of them is a waxy substance,

21
00:01:34,441 --> 00:01:36,440
and the other is a liquid.

22
00:01:36,441 --> 00:01:39,440
If we pop the top, we can actually separate them

23
00:01:39,441 --> 00:01:41,440
and see the differences.

24
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:49,940
Here comes the wax.

25
00:01:49,941 --> 00:01:52,940
The wax is already starting to harden,

26
00:01:52,941 --> 00:01:56,940
and you can see it's just like candle wax that's been melted.

27
00:01:56,941 --> 00:01:59,940
The key to the motion of the blobs within the lamp

28
00:01:59,941 --> 00:02:02,940
is the light bulb in the base.

29
00:02:02,941 --> 00:02:05,940
It keeps the wax from hardening and sinking to the bottom.

30
00:02:05,941 --> 00:02:10,940
The heat melts the wax and then expands it just a little bit,

31
00:02:10,941 --> 00:02:14,940
so that it's less dense than the liquid around it, and it can rise.

32
00:02:14,941 --> 00:02:18,940
As it gets up to the top, it cools, contracts just a little bit,

33
00:02:18,941 --> 00:02:22,752
bit gets more dense and is able to fall back down to the bottom.

34
00:02:22,753 --> 00:02:24,480
And that heating and cooling,

35
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:30,240
expanding, contracting process is what gives this its amazing, mesmerizing look.

36
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:39,080
As lava lamps were making globs of hot wax cool, another fad of the 60s was making pint-sized cars

37
00:02:39,081 --> 00:02:45,560
huge. America's slot car craze began in 1960 with two commercial raceways.

38
00:02:45,561 --> 00:02:51,540
But by 1968, their numbers had swelled to almost 5,000.

39
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:54,060
Some of them were as large as bowling alleys.

40
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:57,020
I remember a track I raced in having nine large tracks.

41
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,840
Early slot car hobbyists like Ed Harris built their own elaborate tracks,

42
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:06,260
and even their own cars.

43
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:10,140
We built them ourselves from a static model kit,

44
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:14,560
and then you found a motor somewhere, usually old train motors,

45
00:03:14,561 --> 00:03:16,920
and then you'd put it together yourself.

46
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:21,320
I had scrounged up gears and tires and so forth,

47
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:22,200
and we went from there.

48
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:27,920
60 slot cars may have resembled real automobiles,

49
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:31,260
but their unusually high power-to-weight ratio

50
00:03:31,261 --> 00:03:34,520
gave them acceleration that could make Mario Andretti drool.

51
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:42,000
Slot cars are the world's fastest motorsport.

52
00:03:42,001 --> 00:03:47,340
A slot car goes from zero to 130 miles an hour in under half a second.

53
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:51,680
There's no real car that comes close.

54
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,160
All slot cars get their power from the track,

55
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:00,840
so they have a motor but no batteries or power source in the car.

56
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,420
Pulling on the trigger of the controller causes electric current

57
00:04:05,421 --> 00:04:07,340
to pass from the power source to the track.

58
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:11,800
From there, rails feed the electricity to the car's motor.

59
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,380
The more the trigger is depressed, the greater the current,

60
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:17,200
and the faster the car drives.

61
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:20,620
In a way, you might say like driving a car,

62
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:23,460
you come into a corner, you've got to slow down,

63
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:26,160
and you either let back off on the throttle,

64
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,000
and then you've got to time the speed going around the curve.

65
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,140
Go too fast, you're going to go off.

66
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:33,960
Yahoo!

67
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:37,820
And if you don't go fast enough, somebody's going to pass you.

68
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:43,300
By the late 60s, slot cars had really reached a peak.

69
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,580
There were thousands of tracks all over the country,

70
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,720
but interest started to wane,

71
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:52,540
so the manufacturers looked for another way to appeal to the racers,

72
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:54,900
and they came up with the Thingy.

73
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:58,340
The Thingy was a dramatically styled car

74
00:04:58,341 --> 00:05:03,380
that they thought would appeal to the youth and racers,

75
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:05,700
and these are some examples.

76
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:07,740
An Asp, like Classic.

77
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:09,520
the Gamma Ray by Classic.

78
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,940
This is a Garvin car and an AMT car.

79
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:16,040
They were popular, especially with kids.

80
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:19,860
In fact, Classic's Manta Ray was the best-selling slot car of all time,

81
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:24,280
but they were very unpopular with those who had hand-built accurate models.

82
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:30,200
So it really split the industry, and many people believe began the collapse.

83
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,280
By the end of the decade, the slot car craze was on its final lap.

84
00:05:35,281 --> 00:05:38,720
But the decade produced other toys so enduring

85
00:05:38,721 --> 00:05:41,420
that they're now hitting middle age,

86
00:05:41,421 --> 00:05:44,320
including everyone's favorite, the Etch-a-Sketch.

87
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:49,720
Its origins date back to the late 50s,

88
00:05:49,721 --> 00:05:52,840
when inventor André Casagnes devised a contraption

89
00:05:52,841 --> 00:05:56,240
in his Paris garage he called Le Creme Magique,

90
00:05:56,241 --> 00:05:58,240
the magic screen.

91
00:05:58,241 --> 00:06:02,240
Etch-a-Sketch, the toy you'll never tire of.

92
00:06:02,241 --> 00:06:05,280
In 1960, the Ohio State of the United States

93
00:06:05,281 --> 00:06:07,780
The Ohio Art Company brought it to America,

94
00:06:07,781 --> 00:06:09,980
renamed it the Etch-a-Sketch,

95
00:06:09,981 --> 00:06:11,880
and sold millions of them.

96
00:06:11,881 --> 00:06:13,780
It was very, very popular in the 60s

97
00:06:13,781 --> 00:06:16,620
because the whole nation was TV crazy.

98
00:06:16,621 --> 00:06:18,820
Everyone was crazy about the television.

99
00:06:18,821 --> 00:06:21,520
And here was a toy that was on the market

100
00:06:21,521 --> 00:06:24,940
that basically looked like a TV, kids could draw on it.

101
00:06:24,941 --> 00:06:28,540
Also, Etch-a-Sketch was one of the first toys

102
00:06:28,541 --> 00:06:31,740
to actually have a commercial on television.

103
00:06:31,741 --> 00:06:33,980
The timeless two-knobbed teledoodler

104
00:06:33,981 --> 00:06:36,740
remained relatively unchanged since its introduction.

105
00:06:36,741 --> 00:06:43,040
If you've ever wondered what's inside, we have the taken apart Etch-A-Sketch here.

106
00:06:43,041 --> 00:06:44,360
And it's really simple.

107
00:06:44,361 --> 00:06:51,060
You've got a stylus, which is what pushes the powder off the glass to make it draw.

108
00:06:51,061 --> 00:06:53,460
We have a pair of rails.

109
00:06:53,461 --> 00:06:58,000
And really what we have is the essence of a computer plotter here.

110
00:06:58,001 --> 00:07:01,900
And then we have the magic ingredient that allows you to erase.

111
00:07:01,901 --> 00:07:05,040
a combination of aluminum beads and aluminum powder.

112
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:09,240
And you might be wondering, why would this aluminum powder stick to the glass?

113
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:11,860
And it's because this aluminum powder sticks to everything.

114
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:14,440
It's really clingy stuff.

115
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:19,600
The left and right knobs enable you to draw horizontal and vertical lines.

116
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:24,260
Most of us have mastered stairs, maybe a house.

117
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:31,320
But Etch-A-Sketch artists like Christoph are etching and or sketching their way to a whole new artistic level.

118
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:39,580
To approach such expertise, you have to master a basic but important technique.

119
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:44,600
Basically, the secret is you have to use both knobs at the same time.

120
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:48,860
If you use the knobs inward like this, you create a diagonal line.

121
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:54,680
Now, if I were to use the knobs side to side in motion, you're creating the other diagonal line.

122
00:07:54,681 --> 00:07:59,600
And then, if you use the force, you can create a circle.

123
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,860
And from there, the possibilities are endless.

124
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:08,380
But be warned.

125
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:12,080
Once your masterpiece is finished, be sure not to shake it.

126
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:18,100
The Etch-a-Sketch isn't the only 60s toy that continues to fascinate us.

127
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:23,340
Case in point, that lively ball with a super-duper bounce.

128
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:28,540
Superball regular made from Zektron with 50,000 pounds of compressed energy.

129
00:08:28,541 --> 00:08:39,060
When the ball was introduced, it was so revolutionary and so high-bouncing that it started to develop rumors around it about where this magical rubber had come from.

130
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:45,940
And, you know, there was stuff about Outer Mongolian specially bred rubber trees and all kinds of crazy stuff.

131
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:52,140
People wondered, is there some kind of magical device or spring or something inside here that gives it its bounce?

132
00:08:52,141 --> 00:08:55,760
but if you go and actually cut it with a knife or a saw and look inside,

133
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:58,800
it's just solid rubber all the way through.

134
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:04,760
The bowl's ingredients are essentially polybutadine rubber,

135
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:08,740
the same kind of rubber used to make most tires, and sulfur.

136
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:14,140
The mixture is forced into a mold, heated to 320 degrees Fahrenheit,

137
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:18,020
and pressurized upwards of 1,000 pounds per square inch,

138
00:09:18,021 --> 00:09:23,300
so that the sulfur molecules cross-link the polybutadine chains.

139
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:29,900
The secret to the supersized bounce is adding about four times more sulfur

140
00:09:29,901 --> 00:09:33,280
than in conventional rubber products, forcing more connections.

141
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:38,320
More cross-links make the superball more resilient,

142
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:42,760
enabling it to bounce back to 92% of the height from which it's dropped,

143
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:45,440
no matter how high that happens to be.

144
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:53,020
But the Super Bowl's real superpower comes when you chuck it.

145
00:09:53,021 --> 00:09:58,020
You could just whack it into the ground and it would go like three stories higher.

146
00:09:58,021 --> 00:10:01,020
So it was shocking how bouncy these balls were.

147
00:10:01,021 --> 00:10:04,020
And that was what made such a huge impression.

148
00:10:04,021 --> 00:10:07,020
Wham-O sold millions and millions and millions of these things

149
00:10:07,021 --> 00:10:14,020
just because there had been no ball like this ever in the history of mankind.

150
00:10:14,021 --> 00:10:17,020
It truly was a Super Bowl.

151
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:22,020
Although kids had a ball playing with innovative toys in the 60s,

152
00:10:22,021 --> 00:10:25,020
the decade was anything but all fun and games.

153
00:10:25,021 --> 00:10:28,020
We were reaching for the moon,

154
00:10:28,021 --> 00:10:31,020
and we relied on one custom camera

155
00:10:31,021 --> 00:10:33,020
to bring the singular moment back to Earth.

156
00:10:33,021 --> 00:10:36,020
That's your piece underneath you.

157
00:10:36,021 --> 00:10:39,020
When American Football League founder Lamar Hunt

158
00:10:39,021 --> 00:10:41,020
watched his children playing with the Super Bowl,

159
00:10:41,021 --> 00:10:44,020
he came up with a new name for the so-called

160
00:10:44,021 --> 00:10:49,100
AFL-NFL World Championship Game, the Super Bowl.

161
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:53,380
60s tech will return on Modern Marvels,

162
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:55,020
here on the History Channel.

163
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:02,980
We call it everything from the boob tube to the idiot box.

164
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:06,740
But there's no denying many of us are totally hooked on television.

165
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:12,060
We can trace our obsession to the beginning of the 60s,

166
00:11:12,061 --> 00:11:16,340
when the evolving medium had found its way into 90% of American homes.

167
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:23,240
It was influencing everything from our shopping choices to politics.

168
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:26,740
The Nixon-Kennedy debates was probably the first time

169
00:11:26,741 --> 00:11:29,960
that television had a significant effect on the outcome of the election,

170
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:33,180
purely because of the way Nixon looked compared to Kennedy.

171
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:41,780
TV's influence blasted to new heights on July 10, 1962.

172
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:44,580
when NASA launched Telstar,

173
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:48,340
the world's first commercial communication satellite.

174
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:55,120
Three Earth stations stood ready to transmit television signals to

175
00:11:55,121 --> 00:11:58,220
and receive signals from the orbiting satellite.

176
00:12:01,740 --> 00:12:04,460
One in Gunhilly Downs in the United Kingdom,

177
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:06,920
one in Plemur-Boudou, France,

178
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:09,500
and one in Andover, Maine.

179
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:14,720
15 hours after Telstar's launch.

180
00:12:14,721 --> 00:12:17,160
Andover technicians crossed their fingers

181
00:12:17,161 --> 00:12:20,000
as they tried it out for the first time.

182
00:12:20,001 --> 00:12:26,440
The first broadcast was made over Telstar 1 from here,

183
00:12:26,441 --> 00:12:29,780
and it was an image of the American flag flying in front

184
00:12:29,781 --> 00:12:33,620
of the control building to Plumard-Badeau, France,

185
00:12:33,621 --> 00:12:37,720
the Earth Station in Europe.

186
00:12:37,721 --> 00:12:39,560
The pioneering event marked the beginning

187
00:12:39,561 --> 00:12:42,320
of the instantaneous global communications revolution.

188
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:47,120
The Andover Earth Station is still in operation today.

189
00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:51,840
Its location is ideally suited for satellite communications.

190
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:57,620
We're in a ball here with the mountains completely surrounding this Earth Station.

191
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:00,380
And the mountains around here in this ball

192
00:13:00,381 --> 00:13:04,480
prevented unwanted radio interference to the satellite signal.

193
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:07,860
And also, we're in the northeastern part of the United States,

194
00:13:07,861 --> 00:13:12,960
which gave us a short loop to Europe for satellite communications.

195
00:13:15,660 --> 00:13:19,360
Andover's equipment may have gone through four decades' worth of upgrades,

196
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:23,020
but its transmission processes remained essentially the same.

197
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:31,220
To transmit TV signals, Andover's engineers in the 60s uplinked broadcasts,

198
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:33,340
then beamed the signals toward Telstar.

199
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:36,680
Sending station identification.

200
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:44,860
TELSTAR

201
00:13:44,861 --> 00:13:46,460
TELSTAR traveled in an elliptical orbit

202
00:13:46,461 --> 00:13:49,460
between 600 and 3,500 miles above Earth.

203
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:53,300
It was equipped with antennas that received the signals.

204
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:56,540
Then TELSTAR amplified them 10 billion times

205
00:13:56,541 --> 00:13:58,960
using traveling wave tube amplifiers

206
00:13:58,961 --> 00:14:01,660
and transmitted them to one of the other Earth stations.

207
00:14:01,661 --> 00:14:07,840
Technicians at those stations were able to move their ground

208
00:14:07,841 --> 00:14:09,840
antenna to optimize the connection.

209
00:14:09,841 --> 00:14:13,840
In the days of Telstar, the antenna that was used for that

210
00:14:13,841 --> 00:14:17,840
was very large and very mobile, because it had to rotate on an

211
00:14:17,841 --> 00:14:20,840
axis and then travel on a track as it followed the satellite

212
00:14:20,841 --> 00:14:21,840
across the sky.

213
00:14:21,841 --> 00:14:24,420
So the antenna would literally move in order to be able to

214
00:14:24,421 --> 00:14:26,760
follow the satellite clear across the horizon from one

215
00:14:26,761 --> 00:14:28,680
side to the other.

216
00:14:28,681 --> 00:14:29,381
other.

217
00:14:29,382 --> 00:14:34,580
Telstar's oblong orbit caused the satellite to be in position for transatlantic

218
00:14:34,581 --> 00:14:38,987
relay for only 20 minutes every two and a half hours.

219
00:14:38,988 --> 00:14:41,980
Today, most communication satellites

220
00:14:41,981 --> 00:14:47,240
are in geosynchronous orbits, matching the speed of Earth's rotation and staying fixed

221
00:14:47,241 --> 00:14:55,811
above one specific point below, enabling them to relay signals 24 hours a day.

222
00:14:55,812 --> 00:14:56,800
After our

223
00:14:56,801 --> 00:15:00,700
TVs capture those signals we take it for granted that we'll be seeing the images

224
00:15:00,701 --> 00:15:07,240
they carry in color but until the 1960s most of us were still staring at screens

225
00:15:07,241 --> 00:15:11,740
filled with black and white color television as we know it today was

226
00:15:11,741 --> 00:15:16,960
invented in the 50s and the first sets were introduced to the public in 1954

227
00:15:16,961 --> 00:15:21,100
but they didn't sell very well there were a number of reasons for this one

228
00:15:21,101 --> 00:15:25,480
was that the technology was crude the sets weren't reliable another was that

229
00:15:25,481 --> 00:15:28,680
but the networks didn't do much color programming

230
00:15:28,681 --> 00:15:32,020
because it was very expensive and they had few viewers.

231
00:15:32,021 --> 00:15:35,240
Finally, in the mid-60s, television took off

232
00:15:35,241 --> 00:15:38,040
because many of the technical problems were solved.

233
00:15:38,041 --> 00:15:40,620
So how did they get color TV to work?

234
00:15:40,621 --> 00:15:41,980
You might be surprised to find out

235
00:15:41,981 --> 00:15:44,380
that the encoded analog signals broadcast

236
00:15:44,381 --> 00:15:48,280
by the networks and local stations weren't in color.

237
00:15:48,281 --> 00:15:50,920
The color on your screen then and now

238
00:15:50,921 --> 00:15:53,540
is made by interpreting wave frequencies

239
00:15:53,541 --> 00:15:56,000
here, inside the television itself.

240
00:15:56,001 --> 00:15:59,380
Television signal comes into the antenna connections here.

241
00:15:59,381 --> 00:16:02,520
It goes into the tuner, which is located up inside,

242
00:16:02,521 --> 00:16:04,520
where the channel is selected.

243
00:16:04,521 --> 00:16:07,820
The signal is then processed by these circuit boards,

244
00:16:07,821 --> 00:16:10,620
which create the black and white signal.

245
00:16:10,621 --> 00:16:14,220
This circuit board is to extract the color signals, red,

246
00:16:14,221 --> 00:16:15,980
green, and blue.

247
00:16:15,981 --> 00:16:19,240
They're then sent to the picture tube.

248
00:16:19,241 --> 00:16:22,020
And you can see I have another tube here.

249
00:16:22,021 --> 00:16:27,020
Inside the tube is three electron guns, one for each color.

250
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:34,420
These guns shoot beams of electrons toward the screen.

251
00:16:34,421 --> 00:16:36,740
The beams pass through a shadow mask,

252
00:16:36,741 --> 00:16:39,840
a plate dotted with thousands of holes.

253
00:16:39,841 --> 00:16:43,080
Behind each hole is a group of three phosphors,

254
00:16:43,081 --> 00:16:48,160
substances that emit light when excited, one for each color.

255
00:16:48,161 --> 00:16:52,340
When the electron beams strike these phosphores, they light up.

256
00:16:52,341 --> 00:16:55,880
If all three beams fire at the same time with the same intensity,

257
00:16:55,881 --> 00:16:59,540
they produce a white dot.

258
00:16:59,541 --> 00:17:03,880
If all three are turned off, they produce a black dot.

259
00:17:03,881 --> 00:17:06,420
Combinations with varying degrees of intensity

260
00:17:06,421 --> 00:17:07,820
create all the other colors.

261
00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:12,820
Pull far enough away from the individual dots,

262
00:17:12,821 --> 00:17:14,320
and you get the big color picture.

263
00:17:14,321 --> 00:17:19,220
The technology won over 60s consumers.

264
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:22,400
Minnie plunked down upwards of $395

265
00:17:22,401 --> 00:17:24,600
for a new 24-inch color set.

266
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:30,880
There was more detail.

267
00:17:31,020 --> 00:17:31,720
There was more to see.

268
00:17:31,721 --> 00:17:32,421
It was more graphic.

269
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:35,560
Something like watching news of the Vietnam War,

270
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:37,880
all of a sudden people could see blood in color,

271
00:17:38,020 --> 00:17:41,040
and that was very striking and very memorable to people

272
00:17:41,041 --> 00:17:43,920
and had an influence on the anti-war movement.

273
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:49,020
Despite the revolution in color TV,

274
00:17:49,620 --> 00:17:51,960
the 60s' most eventful television moment

275
00:17:51,961 --> 00:17:53,800
would be broadcast in black and white.

276
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:57,140
On July 20, 1969,

277
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,700
more than 600 million mesmerized viewers

278
00:18:00,701 --> 00:18:03,840
tuned in to watch man's first steps on the moon.

279
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:06,940
That's one small step for man,

280
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:10,680
one giant leap for mankind.

281
00:18:10,681 --> 00:18:18,420
It was a broadcast television feat that would require an unprecedented technological leap.

282
00:18:20,040 --> 00:18:25,820
It began when Stan Labar, a Westinghouse engineer, was given a seemingly impossible task.

283
00:18:28,380 --> 00:18:33,600
His challenge was to design a compact camera, rugged enough to operate in the extremes of

284
00:18:33,601 --> 00:18:40,160
the moon environment, and transmit the video signal 238,000 miles back to Earth.

285
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:44,200
When we were faced with this, it was rather amazing

286
00:18:44,201 --> 00:18:46,880
as how the group that we pulled together

287
00:18:46,881 --> 00:18:51,720
suddenly became so focused in on this particular problem

288
00:18:51,721 --> 00:18:54,040
unlike anything we had ever done before.

289
00:18:56,080 --> 00:18:59,000
LeBar and his team had to make the camera simple enough

290
00:18:59,001 --> 00:19:01,600
for the astronauts to handle while wearing their spacesuits

291
00:19:01,601 --> 00:19:05,440
and strong enough to withstand lunar temperature extremes

292
00:19:05,441 --> 00:19:10,620
of 250 degrees Fahrenheit to 250 degrees below zero.

293
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:19,860
One of LeBar's most daunting challenges was to make the camera function using extremely limited electrical power.

294
00:19:21,060 --> 00:19:27,720
NASA project leaders had to allocate the lion's share of power to more essential hardware, leaving precious little to spare.

295
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:32,660
They limited the amount of power that we could use on a camera to 7 watts.

296
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:36,720
7 watts is the power from one Christmas bulb.

297
00:19:36,721 --> 00:19:40,720
Power restrictions also limited the bandwidth of the camera's

298
00:19:40,721 --> 00:19:44,720
signal to only 500 kilohertz, one-eighth the bandwidth of a

299
00:19:44,721 --> 00:19:49,720
conventional television signal. This forced the team to come up

300
00:19:49,721 --> 00:19:52,720
with a radically new format for the camera.

301
00:19:52,721 --> 00:19:56,720
Your home television works at 30 frames a second. This one

302
00:19:56,721 --> 00:20:01,720
scans at 10 frames a second. Your home television had 525

303
00:20:01,721 --> 00:20:05,720
lines, scan lines. This one only has 320.

304
00:20:05,721 --> 00:20:13,700
I was informed at the time that this camera would be of a format that had to be converted or it would not be able to be seen on regular broadcast.

305
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:21,520
So we had contracted with the RCA Corporation to build a converter so that when we received this signal at the ground site,

306
00:20:21,660 --> 00:20:26,640
we would be able to convert that prior to transmission back to Houston and release to the public.

307
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:30,380
We had a 210-foot dish in the Mojave Desert.

308
00:20:30,381 --> 00:20:34,620
We had one in Madrid, Spain, and we had one in Australia,

309
00:20:34,621 --> 00:20:38,500
which covered a 360-degree Earth as the Earth turned

310
00:20:38,501 --> 00:20:39,920
and our view of the moon changed.

311
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:44,340
Tranquility Base here. The Eagle has landed.

312
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:49,560
When we landed, at that point, I would say within 30 minutes,

313
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:52,900
it became a realization that this television that was nice

314
00:20:52,901 --> 00:20:56,340
if you could get it but not required became the event,

315
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:59,220
and it became the, quote-unquote, requirement,

316
00:20:59,221 --> 00:21:01,460
we better see the first step on the moon.

317
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:07,400
Apollo 11's camera and the complex transmission and conversion processes

318
00:21:07,401 --> 00:21:09,080
worked almost without a hitch.

319
00:21:09,820 --> 00:21:13,740
The images lost a fraction of their clarity on their way from Australia to Houston.

320
00:21:16,020 --> 00:21:18,420
But even though the image broadcast was blurry,

321
00:21:19,260 --> 00:21:23,940
it captured Neil Armstrong live, making a giant leap for mankind.

322
00:21:23,941 --> 00:21:28,940
Ironically, NASA has lost the tapes of the cleaner transmission beamed to Australia.

323
00:21:28,941 --> 00:21:36,940
All that remains of it is this filmed image of the Australian monitor, shot by a technician at the relay station.

324
00:21:36,941 --> 00:21:43,940
But the lunar camera has had a lasting impact, raising the bar of TV technology.

325
00:21:43,941 --> 00:21:45,180
of TV technology.

326
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:51,500
60s TV also introduced us to the car that captured the decade,

327
00:21:51,980 --> 00:21:55,740
a stylish beauty that many Ford execs never wanted to make.

328
00:21:57,900 --> 00:22:02,120
At the beginning of the 60s, an ultrasonic remote control

329
00:22:02,121 --> 00:22:05,220
added about 30% to the cost of a television set,

330
00:22:05,740 --> 00:22:08,760
or $100 for a $350 set,

331
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:12,340
the equivalent of roughly $700 today.

332
00:22:13,940 --> 00:22:17,900
60s tech will return on Modern Marvels here on the History

333
00:22:17,901 --> 00:22:18,601
Channel.

334
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:25,980
There's just something about a 60s Mustang.

335
00:22:29,300 --> 00:22:33,720
If any car could claim the decade, this was it.

336
00:22:33,721 --> 00:22:36,500
More than 40 years since its introduction,

337
00:22:36,501 --> 00:22:38,900
this legend of the American auto industry still

338
00:22:38,901 --> 00:22:41,240
has the ability to turn heads and trigger envy.

339
00:22:43,940 --> 00:22:47,380
It's no wonder everyone had to have one in the 60s.

340
00:22:48,560 --> 00:22:50,680
And why many still want one today.

341
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:57,860
In the early 60s, Ford General Manager Lee Iacocca realized that the boomer generation was coming of age.

342
00:22:58,240 --> 00:23:01,020
An untapped market crying out for a car to call its own.

343
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:04,360
In 1964, Ford unveiled the Mustang.

344
00:23:05,020 --> 00:23:07,880
It caught the entire United States' fancy.

345
00:23:08,060 --> 00:23:10,680
Everyone wanted a Mustang. Everyone wanted to be seen in a Mustang.

346
00:23:10,681 --> 00:23:15,500
And sure enough, within the first two years, they sold over a million cars.

347
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:17,880
The first year sales had set records.

348
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:20,100
No other car had sold in those numbers.

349
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:26,140
It was all because the car was the right car, the right price, the right time, and it had the right look.

350
00:23:27,700 --> 00:23:30,840
Ironically, this automotive icon was almost the car that wasn't.

351
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:39,140
Ford executives had to be convinced by Iacocca that they needed his car instead of their Falcon.

352
00:23:39,141 --> 00:23:43,400
Well, when he first pitched the idea of this youthful young car,

353
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:45,400
it was basically summarily thrown out.

354
00:23:45,820 --> 00:23:49,120
And the answer was no, no, over and over again, no.

355
00:23:49,380 --> 00:23:52,240
So that's when he decided, let's leave the glass house.

356
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:54,980
Let's get out of there on Michigan Avenue, drive up the street,

357
00:23:55,260 --> 00:23:57,740
stop in a little motel called the Fairlane Motel,

358
00:23:57,840 --> 00:24:00,880
rent a room, and think outside the box, if you will.

359
00:24:00,920 --> 00:24:03,480
I mean, go there, go off campus, let's think this through.

360
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:05,380
And sure enough, that's where the Mustang was born.

361
00:24:05,381 --> 00:24:09,520
Although Iacocca's design team did just that,

362
00:24:09,700 --> 00:24:12,480
they also used the Dowdy Falcon as their starting point.

363
00:24:13,940 --> 00:24:17,860
This was a typical Falcon, 1963.

364
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:22,480
The Mustang is based on the car except for the styling.

365
00:24:25,680 --> 00:24:28,260
Basically the same platform as the Falcon.

366
00:24:28,440 --> 00:24:32,760
It's a unit body car, which means it's all one unit.

367
00:24:32,761 --> 00:24:35,940
It doesn't have a separate frame that the body bolts to.

368
00:24:36,300 --> 00:24:39,720
And then they added these torque boxes out here,

369
00:24:40,040 --> 00:24:43,380
which again is just steel plates,

370
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:46,720
but it added strength to the frame of the car

371
00:24:46,721 --> 00:24:48,460
and kept it from flexing.

372
00:24:50,940 --> 00:24:54,220
Ford designers took the Falcon's platform and engine assembly,

373
00:24:54,600 --> 00:24:56,220
then shortened the wheelbase,

374
00:24:57,040 --> 00:24:58,100
lowered the seating position,

375
00:24:58,760 --> 00:24:59,860
reduced the trunk space,

376
00:24:59,861 --> 00:25:01,420
and elongated the hood.

377
00:25:02,760 --> 00:25:06,920
They also gave it a well-appointed interior

378
00:25:06,921 --> 00:25:10,080
and wrapped it in its sleek signature body style.

379
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:12,460
When they went ahead and they decided

380
00:25:12,461 --> 00:25:15,440
they were going to build a four-seat sporty car,

381
00:25:15,640 --> 00:25:17,660
there was a lot of debate about what the name should be.

382
00:25:18,540 --> 00:25:20,320
Some people wanted to call it a Cougar,

383
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:23,520
and the name Torino was tossed about.

384
00:25:24,820 --> 00:25:27,060
The debate ended when they turned their eyes

385
00:25:27,061 --> 00:25:28,280
on this unusual car.

386
00:25:29,040 --> 00:25:32,860
This car in front of me is known as the Mustang One.

387
00:25:33,420 --> 00:25:39,060
This little car came out in 1962 as what they call a concept car.

388
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:42,280
It was the first Ford car to bear the name Mustang.

389
00:25:43,980 --> 00:25:47,240
And it was a sensation when it came out.

390
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,100
Ford really just borrowed the name Mustang from this car.

391
00:25:51,101 --> 00:25:59,120
and eventually they decided to take advantage of all the good publicity that this little car had

392
00:25:59,121 --> 00:26:08,800
generated to call a production car a mustang in the spring of 1964 iacocca's brainchild finally

393
00:26:08,801 --> 00:26:15,220
hit the market and when it finally hit it took the country by storm there are great stories about

394
00:26:15,221 --> 00:26:20,380
people staying outside their dealerships and crowds that that couldn't be contained every car that was

395
00:26:20,381 --> 00:26:22,580
in the dealership on the first day selling out.

396
00:26:24,620 --> 00:26:27,120
Boasting a starting price of around $2,500,

397
00:26:28,060 --> 00:26:30,140
the Mustang appealed to just about everyone.

398
00:26:30,920 --> 00:26:33,080
You could buy a Mustang as a hard top,

399
00:26:33,780 --> 00:26:36,400
a fastback, or a convertible.

400
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:39,900
One of the keys to the Mustang's success

401
00:26:39,901 --> 00:26:41,900
was that you could make it your own.

402
00:26:43,060 --> 00:26:46,940
And it's very hard today to find two of these early Mustangs

403
00:26:46,941 --> 00:26:47,980
that are exactly alike.

404
00:26:50,380 --> 00:26:54,820
There was even a Mustang for drivers who demanded something brawnier under the hood.

405
00:26:55,540 --> 00:26:57,660
This is a 66 GT350.

406
00:26:58,700 --> 00:27:05,780
Carroll Shelby took the regular Mustang in 65 on up and modified it to be a little more performance oriented.

407
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:14,140
What he did was a few modifications to the car that you normally don't have on a regular fastback.

408
00:27:14,141 --> 00:27:22,160
Modifications included a V8 racing engine and a special intake manifold and carburetor

409
00:27:22,161 --> 00:27:26,680
to increase its power from 271 to 306 horsepower.

410
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:32,620
Most people would prefer to have these if they're a little more speed-oriented, a little

411
00:27:32,621 --> 00:27:36,180
more racing, a little more prestige.

412
00:27:36,181 --> 00:27:39,540
It's more of a rarer car than a regular Mustang.

413
00:27:39,880 --> 00:27:41,560
Shelby didn't make that many of them.

414
00:27:44,140 --> 00:27:46,460
Not everyone could own one of these wild Mustangs,

415
00:27:46,840 --> 00:27:48,640
but that didn't mean you couldn't drive one.

416
00:27:49,060 --> 00:27:52,740
Hertz realized that there could be a real marketing opportunity here

417
00:27:52,741 --> 00:27:56,520
for renting cars to people who couldn't afford to buy these things

418
00:27:56,521 --> 00:27:58,620
but wanted to be able to drive one.

419
00:27:58,621 --> 00:28:04,620
And they worked with Shelby, created a number of special GT350s.

420
00:28:04,640 --> 00:28:07,240
They were painted black and gold, which were Hertz's colors.

421
00:28:07,680 --> 00:28:12,400
They were called GT350 Hs, and you could go in and rent one.

422
00:28:12,401 --> 00:28:16,000
People used to come in and rent them for the weekend.

423
00:28:16,140 --> 00:28:17,740
That's why they called it a rent eraser.

424
00:28:18,040 --> 00:28:22,700
And they'd take them out to the drag strips or they'd take them out to the oval tracks and they'd race them.

425
00:28:23,180 --> 00:28:27,860
And then they'd bring them back Monday and they had a good weekend and it didn't cost much.

426
00:28:28,700 --> 00:28:37,040
Then guys started deciding that, hey, we can take these engines out of the Shelby's

427
00:28:37,041 --> 00:28:42,500
and put our less powerful engines back in them

428
00:28:42,501 --> 00:28:45,520
and use these in our everyday cars,

429
00:28:46,380 --> 00:28:48,140
and Hertz didn't know the difference.

430
00:28:48,280 --> 00:28:51,220
They were not car aficionados.

431
00:28:51,380 --> 00:28:52,640
They were just in the rental business.

432
00:28:54,920 --> 00:28:57,180
Today, you can still rent your own racer,

433
00:28:57,760 --> 00:29:00,360
as Hertz is once again offering the Shelby to its customers.

434
00:29:02,300 --> 00:29:05,080
What the Mustang did for the 60s car industry,

435
00:29:05,081 --> 00:29:08,080
This push-button wonder would do for communication.

436
00:29:08,081 --> 00:29:12,080
And this humming watch would do for timekeeping.

437
00:29:14,080 --> 00:29:19,080
In 1999, a man in Tennessee applied for a license to marry his Ford Mustang.

438
00:29:19,081 --> 00:29:22,080
He listed his fiancée's birthplace as Detroit,

439
00:29:22,081 --> 00:29:27,080
her father's name as Henry Ford, and her blood type as 10W40.

440
00:29:27,081 --> 00:29:29,080
His application was rejected.

441
00:29:29,081 --> 00:29:32,080
60s Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

442
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:43,520
The 60s was the first decade to rock and roll from beginning to end.

443
00:29:44,040 --> 00:29:46,420
And you could take the dance party wherever you went.

444
00:29:48,040 --> 00:29:50,220
No one had ever had that ability before.

445
00:29:50,360 --> 00:29:52,240
You could walk around and listen to your music,

446
00:29:52,241 --> 00:29:55,000
and even though it sounded like crap, no one really cared.

447
00:29:55,180 --> 00:29:58,660
It was so amazing to be able to do it that you were happy about it.

448
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:04,020
The gizmo that made the transistor radio possible was, of course,

449
00:30:04,021 --> 00:30:05,213
the transistor.

450
00:30:05,214 --> 00:30:10,780
In the late 60s, this tiny electrical wonder was making a huge impact.

451
00:30:11,660 --> 00:30:16,772
Transistors are a pivotal technology, like a wheel or, you know, fire.

452
00:30:16,773 --> 00:30:18,160
They are so amazing

453
00:30:18,161 --> 00:30:24,236
in electronics, in computers. We have here what are called discrete transistors.

454
00:30:24,237 --> 00:30:24,937
These are

455
00:30:24,938 --> 00:30:26,867
probably from the 60s and 70s.

456
00:30:26,868 --> 00:30:30,760
They're called discrete not because they don't tell secrets,

457
00:30:30,761 --> 00:30:36,180
but because they're individual transistors, each one packaged in a separate header,

458
00:30:36,320 --> 00:30:38,180
as they're called in the business.

459
00:30:39,920 --> 00:30:45,180
The transistor had been invented in 1947 by three scientists at Bell Laboratories,

460
00:30:45,640 --> 00:30:49,000
looking for a replacement for inefficient and bulky vacuum tubes.

461
00:30:49,420 --> 00:30:53,440
They found out that they could control the current through this piece of crystal

462
00:30:53,441 --> 00:30:55,560
by the voltages they put on the wires.

463
00:30:55,640 --> 00:30:59,600
And they could put just a little bit of voltage, a little bit of current, on the wires,

464
00:30:59,601 --> 00:31:03,240
and they would cause a large current change through the crystal.

465
00:31:05,700 --> 00:31:07,820
In the case of a transistor radio,

466
00:31:08,360 --> 00:31:12,820
a weak signal travels from the antenna through various components to a transistor.

467
00:31:13,860 --> 00:31:15,100
There it's amplified.

468
00:31:17,200 --> 00:31:20,020
The amplified signal is then directed to the speaker

469
00:31:20,021 --> 00:31:22,700
that translates the signal into audible sound.

470
00:31:22,701 --> 00:31:30,540
Electronics manufacturers of the 60s wasted little time capitalizing on the new technology.

471
00:31:32,460 --> 00:31:35,500
Transistor radios hit the market by the mid-1950s.

472
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:39,980
But it wasn't until Japanese companies like Sony streamlined production

473
00:31:39,981 --> 00:31:43,180
that they became as affordable as they were desirable.

474
00:31:43,920 --> 00:31:49,780
In the 60s, Hong Kong and Japan took over the transistor marketplace

475
00:31:49,781 --> 00:31:52,900
and began producing really cheap radios

476
00:31:52,901 --> 00:31:56,220
to the point where they dropped from $40 to $30 to $20

477
00:31:56,221 --> 00:31:58,660
down to $10, and then everybody could have one,

478
00:31:58,700 --> 00:32:00,100
and everybody did want one.

479
00:32:01,980 --> 00:32:05,580
As transistors helped give our radios a makeover in the 60s,

480
00:32:05,940 --> 00:32:08,380
they did the same for that other electronic necessity,

481
00:32:09,220 --> 00:32:09,960
the telephone.

482
00:32:11,660 --> 00:32:16,460
Until 1964, all home telephones featured a rotary pulse dial,

483
00:32:16,461 --> 00:32:21,760
where the series of pulses corresponded to each of the digits on the phone.

484
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:31,020
Consumers at the 1964 New York World's Fair got a sneak preview of things to come

485
00:32:31,021 --> 00:32:33,720
as touch-tone phones were put to the test.

486
00:32:33,721 --> 00:32:37,100
Hi, this is the Bell System's new touch-tone dialing.

487
00:32:37,440 --> 00:32:40,600
With this indicator, you see how many seconds you save the new way.

488
00:32:40,940 --> 00:32:41,640
Let's try it!

489
00:32:41,641 --> 00:32:42,380
Okay, I'll raise you.

490
00:32:42,500 --> 00:32:43,200
Ready?

491
00:32:43,201 --> 00:32:43,901
Go.

492
00:32:44,580 --> 00:32:46,140
They were an instant hit.

493
00:32:46,460 --> 00:32:49,940
and literally set the tone for the future of telephone dialing.

494
00:32:49,941 --> 00:32:51,200
I need you.

495
00:32:51,201 --> 00:32:55,460
If you look at this phone, you can actually see how simple it is.

496
00:32:55,461 --> 00:33:00,440
When you push a button, you're generating, like, a contact

497
00:33:00,441 --> 00:33:02,780
between two pieces of metal here,

498
00:33:02,781 --> 00:33:07,620
and that contact is hooking to a tone producer,

499
00:33:07,621 --> 00:33:11,520
and there's four tone producers down this line,

500
00:33:11,521 --> 00:33:13,280
and there's three across here.

501
00:33:13,281 --> 00:33:20,480
When you push the button, two separate tones are combined, and that's what's sent down the line.

502
00:33:22,320 --> 00:33:31,567
The reason they wanted to use two tones was that they didn't want your normal voice or any whistles in the background or something to get picked up and interpreted by

503
00:33:31,568 --> 00:33:32,268
the system.

504
00:33:33,920 --> 00:33:41,380
In the 40-plus years since its inception, the touchtone phone has saved each of us an average of over 400 hours of dialing.

505
00:33:43,280 --> 00:33:48,280
Innovations in the 60s not only revolutionized the way we made phone calls,

506
00:33:48,281 --> 00:33:52,280
but also changed the way we kept time.

507
00:33:52,281 --> 00:33:57,280
For 300 years, watches ticked.

508
00:33:57,281 --> 00:34:01,280
But Accutron had a fluid motion and hummed.

509
00:34:01,281 --> 00:34:06,280
What I have here is one of the original Accutrons from the 1960s.

510
00:34:06,281 --> 00:34:10,280
It was originally launched in the 1960s by Boulevard Corporation.

511
00:34:10,281 --> 00:34:14,020
It was the first revolution in 300 years in timekeeping.

512
00:34:14,620 --> 00:34:16,960
Prior to that, all watches were mechanical.

513
00:34:17,360 --> 00:34:21,300
They had a balance wheel, which rotated from three to five times per second.

514
00:34:22,560 --> 00:34:24,300
The Accutron was different.

515
00:34:24,880 --> 00:34:28,580
Instead of a rotating balance wheel to create the watch's constant regular motion,

516
00:34:28,980 --> 00:34:30,820
it used an oscillating tuning fork.

517
00:34:32,620 --> 00:34:36,580
This is actually a tuning fork that was in one of the movements,

518
00:34:36,581 --> 00:34:42,580
and what happened is you had a circuit that created an electromagnetic field.

519
00:34:45,280 --> 00:34:48,740
The electromagnetic field created by copper coils

520
00:34:48,741 --> 00:34:53,300
vibrated the magnets at the end of the tuning fork 360 times per second.

521
00:34:55,160 --> 00:34:59,000
As the tuning fork vibrated, an index finger attached to it

522
00:34:59,001 --> 00:35:02,340
pushed and retreated, forcing a gear wheel to turn.

523
00:35:02,341 --> 00:35:08,220
A tuning fork was not new to timekeeping.

524
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:12,900
Actually, in 1866, there was a patent issued to Breguet

525
00:35:12,901 --> 00:35:16,320
to put a tuning fork into a clock movement.

526
00:35:16,780 --> 00:35:19,960
But it wasn't until the miniature of the transistor

527
00:35:19,961 --> 00:35:23,280
that it was capable of being put into a watch

528
00:35:23,281 --> 00:35:24,620
that one could wear on the wrist.

529
00:35:26,720 --> 00:35:29,500
Bulova engineers had begun working on the concept

530
00:35:29,501 --> 00:35:32,300
of an electronically driven wristwatch in 1953.

531
00:35:32,340 --> 00:35:40,520
After years of effort ticked by, Bulova introduced the Accutron to consumers in October 1960.

532
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:47,480
Consumers purchased over 4 million Accutrons until production stopped in 1977.

533
00:35:49,600 --> 00:35:53,820
Surprisingly, it still attracted buyers well after quartz crystal technology,

534
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:59,620
with its ability to oscillate at over 32,000 vibrations per second, had trumped it in accuracy.

535
00:36:00,520 --> 00:36:07,540
People are very passionate about their Accutron, not only because it is a historic item, but it's also a collectible.

536
00:36:09,860 --> 00:36:14,060
Time was running out on the 60s, but the trip wasn't over,

537
00:36:14,460 --> 00:36:19,340
as the freewheeling counterculture still had its technological mark to leave on the decade.

538
00:36:22,080 --> 00:36:28,760
The 9-volt battery was introduced in 1956, specifically to power portable transistor radios,

539
00:36:28,761 --> 00:36:34,680
which required a higher voltage than supplied by conventional 1.5-volt batteries of the time.

540
00:36:35,420 --> 00:36:39,600
60s tech will return on Modern Marvels here on the History Channel.

541
00:36:45,200 --> 00:36:50,740
Among its many signature features, the 60s had a distinctive sound all its own.

542
00:36:53,220 --> 00:36:56,400
A sound that expressed the rebellion of a new counterculture

543
00:36:56,401 --> 00:37:00,060
that felt free to tune in, turn on, and drop out.

544
00:37:02,040 --> 00:37:04,560
This restless generation gathered to create

545
00:37:04,561 --> 00:37:08,020
the ultimate youth event of the 60s, Woodstock.

546
00:37:09,600 --> 00:37:12,740
The sound guru who made the music blast at Woodstock

547
00:37:12,741 --> 00:37:14,780
was audio engineer Bill Hanley.

548
00:37:18,480 --> 00:37:21,260
Hanley still has the audio artifacts from the festival

549
00:37:21,261 --> 00:37:23,060
tucked away in his backyard sheds.

550
00:37:23,620 --> 00:37:25,800
These are the mixes that we used at Woodstock.

551
00:37:26,400 --> 00:37:28,240
and this one and that one.

552
00:37:28,241 --> 00:37:30,100
These are different style ones that we had.

553
00:37:32,340 --> 00:37:34,080
Hanley's greatest challenge at Woodstock

554
00:37:34,081 --> 00:37:36,120
was to make the music audible to the audience

555
00:37:36,121 --> 00:37:37,340
furthest from the stage.

556
00:37:38,840 --> 00:37:40,840
I actually picked the site for Woodstock

557
00:37:40,841 --> 00:37:44,260
and designed and laid it out so that the crowd went up

558
00:37:44,261 --> 00:37:46,880
in an amphitheater-type operation.

559
00:37:46,881 --> 00:37:50,360
I designed the walls to funnel everybody in

560
00:37:50,361 --> 00:37:52,660
so that they would be in the range of the speakers.

561
00:37:52,661 --> 00:38:01,760
We never expected that big a crowd, so what we ended up with is people everywhere.

562
00:38:01,761 --> 00:38:07,180
The audio requirements of large rock festivals compelled sound engineers to mic the instruments,

563
00:38:07,181 --> 00:38:11,040
an innovation of the 60s that's now commonplace.

564
00:38:11,041 --> 00:38:12,600
That's the change.

565
00:38:12,601 --> 00:38:15,700
That's the big change in technology that we used.

566
00:38:15,701 --> 00:38:17,320
So I mic'd all the drums.

567
00:38:17,321 --> 00:38:23,560
I might tom-tom, the floor tom-tom, the kick drum, the snare, the hi-hat, the cymbals up above.

568
00:38:24,140 --> 00:38:30,340
I had five or six mics on the drums, which was, you know, not really done until 68, 69,

569
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:33,100
except for people like Bill on large shows.

570
00:38:35,160 --> 00:38:38,960
Before the 60s, engineers had purposely avoided micing the instruments,

571
00:38:39,140 --> 00:38:43,260
since gathering too many mics on stage created a greater potential for feedback.

572
00:38:43,261 --> 00:38:48,720
Feedback is that annoying noise we've all heard,

573
00:38:49,320 --> 00:38:52,100
caused when an audio loop generates between the microphone

574
00:38:52,101 --> 00:38:54,180
and the loudspeaker carrying its signal.

575
00:38:57,320 --> 00:39:00,160
But a solution to the problem arrived with the introduction

576
00:39:00,161 --> 00:39:04,540
of the Shure Unidyne 3 family of microphones in 1959.

577
00:39:06,160 --> 00:39:08,720
This microphone was the most important advancement

578
00:39:08,721 --> 00:39:12,820
for the public address systems and sound reinforcement systems of the 60s era.

579
00:39:13,260 --> 00:39:18,980
Before this generation of microphones, the most popular mics picked up sound in a wide

580
00:39:18,981 --> 00:39:21,260
pattern around the front.

581
00:39:21,261 --> 00:39:28,020
But this new end-firing model picked up sound in a narrower pattern from the top end.

582
00:39:28,021 --> 00:39:33,020
It's like a Christmas tree, like that, upside down, facing in.

583
00:39:33,021 --> 00:39:36,540
And people, if you talk in that way, you've got a good pickup.

584
00:39:36,541 --> 00:39:40,020
If you talk in this way, you've got very little pickup.

585
00:39:40,021 --> 00:39:47,340
The compact conical end-firing design, with its innovative acoustical chambers, gave it

586
00:39:47,341 --> 00:39:51,200
incomparable feedback control.

587
00:39:51,201 --> 00:39:57,420
Engineers could now put numerous mics on stage and use massive speakers to amplify the sound,

588
00:39:57,421 --> 00:40:06,800
powering the rise of rock concerts and festivals as we know them today.

589
00:40:06,801 --> 00:40:10,040
60s rock shows were not only an auditory spectacle,

590
00:40:10,041 --> 00:40:11,540
but also a visual one.

591
00:40:14,940 --> 00:40:17,280
Visual artists like Joshua White

592
00:40:17,281 --> 00:40:19,420
were the men behind the psychedelic curtain.

593
00:40:19,421 --> 00:40:22,680
What was there before light shows came on the scene?

594
00:40:22,681 --> 00:40:24,980
Nothing, there was nothing there.

595
00:40:24,981 --> 00:40:26,560
It was a completely different time.

596
00:40:26,561 --> 00:40:30,060
It was the 50s and everybody, everything was gray.

597
00:40:30,061 --> 00:40:33,360
And you went to a concert and you sat and watched the band

598
00:40:33,361 --> 00:40:35,360
and there would maybe be a curtain in the background

599
00:40:35,361 --> 00:40:36,740
and maybe there'd be a follow spot.

600
00:40:37,220 --> 00:40:38,780
But at the end of the 60s,

601
00:40:38,820 --> 00:40:40,800
the music began to really change and grow,

602
00:40:40,900 --> 00:40:45,700
and it took on a very distinctive American, psychedelic flavor.

603
00:40:46,180 --> 00:40:48,180
And there was nothing to look at.

604
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:51,560
It was brilliant to listen to, and there was nothing to look at.

605
00:40:51,640 --> 00:40:54,220
And there was a window of opportunity

606
00:40:54,221 --> 00:40:56,600
for the creation of the psychedelic light show,

607
00:40:56,601 --> 00:40:59,580
which was something you looked at while you listened to the music.

608
00:41:01,740 --> 00:41:04,140
So how did light show producers like Joshua White

609
00:41:04,141 --> 00:41:06,480
create this larger-than-life psychedelic art?

610
00:41:08,080 --> 00:41:11,340
This is the heart and soul

611
00:41:11,341 --> 00:41:13,700
of what the original light shows were.

612
00:41:14,040 --> 00:41:15,880
It's an overhead projector,

613
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:18,060
the kind that we used in high school.

614
00:41:19,080 --> 00:41:21,120
And then we took clock faces,

615
00:41:21,220 --> 00:41:23,940
and this is literally a face-off of a clock.

616
00:41:24,300 --> 00:41:27,180
And then we began with the first and most primary thing

617
00:41:27,181 --> 00:41:28,980
that everybody remembers about a light show,

618
00:41:28,981 --> 00:41:30,960
which was the liquid part of the light show.

619
00:41:32,320 --> 00:41:33,600
So we took water,

620
00:41:33,601 --> 00:41:40,100
and then we took oil, common, ordinary mineral oil,

621
00:41:40,101 --> 00:41:45,080
and we would squirt it on top of the water.

622
00:41:45,081 --> 00:41:47,140
And this is the great secret reveal

623
00:41:47,141 --> 00:41:51,580
that psychedelic light shows is that oil and water don't mix.

624
00:41:51,581 --> 00:41:55,020
And then we would add a little bit of color.

625
00:41:55,021 --> 00:41:58,300
Now you can add other elements to it

626
00:41:58,301 --> 00:42:01,600
so that you can take something as simple as alcohol

627
00:42:01,601 --> 00:42:03,220
and squirt the alcohol in,

628
00:42:03,500 --> 00:42:05,620
and the plate begins to take on

629
00:42:05,621 --> 00:42:07,380
psychedelic qualities of its own.

630
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:10,580
Everybody thinks I'm doing this amazing thing,

631
00:42:10,581 --> 00:42:11,720
but I'm really not.

632
00:42:11,820 --> 00:42:13,680
All I'm doing is supervising.

633
00:42:15,900 --> 00:42:18,880
Light show teams had a virtual arsenal of tools

634
00:42:18,881 --> 00:42:19,620
they could incorporate,

635
00:42:20,240 --> 00:42:21,380
like color wheels,

636
00:42:22,460 --> 00:42:23,160
filters,

637
00:42:23,440 --> 00:42:24,480
and light sticks.

638
00:42:28,760 --> 00:42:30,860
All so that the youth of the 60s

639
00:42:30,861 --> 00:42:34,860
in their own psychedelic fantasies.

640
00:42:34,861 --> 00:42:39,860
The 60s could be described as the nation's bipolar adolescence.

641
00:42:39,861 --> 00:42:42,860
We changed during those 10 tumultuous years,

642
00:42:42,861 --> 00:42:46,860
lost our innocence, and developed many technologies

643
00:42:46,861 --> 00:42:50,860
for the decades to come.

